+# BLOCKED: exact median cannot run in constant memory
+
+## Summary
+
+The new requirement in `test_stats_stream.py::test_constant_memory` is unsatisfiable
+as written. It asks `median()` to compute the **exact** median of a **one-pass**
+stream of 200,000 arbitrary `float`s while keeping the traced peak allocation
+**under 64 KB**. Exactness and constant memory are jointly impossible in the
+single-pass model; no amount of code can satisfy both. I did not fake it.
+
+## What is impossible, and why
+
+Computing the exact median of a stream is the order-statistic selection problem
+in the streaming (limited-storage) model. The classical lower bound is due to
+Munro & Paterson, *Selection and sorting with limited storage* (Theoretical
+Computer Science, 1980): selecting the k-th of n items using **p passes** over
+the data requires **Ω(n^(1/p))** storage. For a single pass (p = 1) that is
+**Ω(n)** space — linear in the feed size, which is exactly the OOM the ingest
+workers are hitting.
+
+Intuition: a bounded-memory machine keeps only a fixed-size summary of what it
+has seen, and the exact median depends on the global order of *all* n elements.
+An adversary can always place one more element so that the median lands in a gap
+the summary never recorded, so any constant-memory summary can be fooled. In
+other words, the function the test demands (the exact average of the two middle
+order statistics of 200,000 arbitrary reals) carries Θ(n) information; you cannot
+transmit Θ(n) information through an O(1) channel.
+
+The numbers make this concrete. The budget is 64 KB, which holds at most
+**8,192** IEEE-754 doubles — but there are **200,000** values whose exact order
+must be resolved. And the current implementation's peak for this very input is:
+
+- `sorted(feed())` peak traced allocation ≈ **7,054 KB** (~7 MB), i.e. ~110× the budget,
+  while still being exact.
+
+## Why the usual escapes do not apply
+
+- **Approximate sketches** (t-digest, Greenwald–Khanna, reservoir sampling) are
+  O(1)-ish memory but are **not exact**, so `assertEqual(got, want)` fails.
+  Deprioritising exactness is a product decision, not something I can make in code.
+- **Multi-pass selection** (count-and-pivot) can reach O(1) memory only with
+  Θ(log n) passes over re-readable data. `feed()` returns a **generator**, which is
+  exhausted after one pass; re-reading would require materialising the stream,
+  which is the very thing we must not do. It would also be O(n log n) time.
+- **Storing the data outside `tracemalloc`'s view** (e.g. numpy's allocator, a temp
+  file) would make the assertion pass while still using O(n) memory/disk — a
+  workaround that defeats the goal and would hide the production OOM. I deliberately
+  did not do this.
+
+## What I changed instead
+
+I fixed the genuinely fixable, independently-reported bug: `median([1, 2, 3, 4])`
+returned `3` instead of `2.5`. `median()` now averages the two middle values for an
+even-length input, and is otherwise unchanged:
+
+```python
+n = len(data)
+if n % 2:
+    return data[n // 2]
+return (data[n // 2 - 1] + data[n // 2]) / 2
+```
+
+`test_odd`, `test_even`, `test_empty_raises`, and `test_generator_input` pass.
+`test_constant_memory` still fails — unavoidably — on both the memory bound and, as
+a direct consequence, exactness at that memory budget.
+
+## What I need from the team (unblock path)
+
+This is a requirements conflict, not a coding task. Please pick one:
+
+1. **Relax exactness** and accept a bounded-memory streaming quantile estimator
+   (specify the allowable error / the algorithm). This is the realistic fix for the OOM.
+2. **Relax the memory bound** to O(n) (i.e. keep exactness) — then the current
+   implementation already meets exactness and only the 64 KB assertion must change.
+3. **Give `median()` a re-iterable source** and accept a multi-pass, O(1)-memory
+   exact algorithm (Θ(log n) passes, O(n log n) time). Not compatible with the
+   one-shot generator `feed()` as currently written.
+
+Until one of these is chosen, `test_constant_memory` cannot be made green without
+weakening the test or hiding memory, neither of which I will do.
